          "BIOS and UEFI are the firmware interfaces that initialise all this hardware and load the OS; UEFI is the modern, more secure replacement for legacy BIOS. Accessing either is done by repeatedly pressing a manufacturer-specific key immediately after power-on — commonly F2 or Delete, F10 on many HP systems, or Esc on some ASUS systems — before the boot loader ever takes over. From that setup screen you can reorder boot devices, enable Secure Boot, or push a firmware update, all before the operating system itself loads.",
        keyPoints: [
          "GPU: specialised parallel processor for graphics/rendering, also used for password cracking (Hashcat) due to its parallelism",
