        week: 3,
        description:
          "Linux is a family of open-source operating systems built on the Linux kernel, created by Linus Torvalds and first released 17 September 1991 — built specifically because there was no free, working kernel available for the GNU project (a Unix-compatible OS made entirely of free software) at the time. A Linux 'distro' bundles the kernel with software and a package manager into a complete OS; every distro shares the same underlying kernel but differs in bundled tooling and philosophy. Linux is the default choice for servers and cloud infrastructure because of its performance, low resource usage, and greater customisability compared to Windows — it is also the kernel underneath Android and most IoT devices, and is generally considered more secure than proprietary alternatives.",
        keyPoints: [
          "A distro = kernel + software + package manager, e.g. Kali (pentesting, XFCE desktop, preinstalled offensive tools), Mint (lightweight, beginner-friendly, good on old hardware), Ubuntu (Debian-derived, servers, beginner-friendly), Arch (minimal, fully customisable down to the bootloader)",
          "Created because there was no free, working kernel available for the GNU project at the time",
          "Used across servers, cloud, IoT, and Android — considered more secure and customisable than Windows for these use cases",
          "Kali Linux ships pre-loaded with the pentesting tools used throughout this programme (Nmap, Burp, Metasploit, Hashcat)",
          "Delivers strong performance on both desktops and servers with comparatively low resource usage versus Windows",
        ],
        terminalCommands: ["tree / -L 1"],
      },
      {
        id: "m3-linux-2",
        moduleId: "m3-linux",
